# Large binary art goes to LFS. Everything here is a file git would otherwise
# store a fresh copy of on every edit, and these get edited: a model re-export
# rewrites all seven megabytes of it.
#
# The rule for what belongs here is size and opacity together. A .glb is both —
# megabytes, and a diff nobody can read. The panel bezel and the logo are
# neither, so they stay in git where a `git show` still works on them.
*.glb filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# .blend is the same case as .glb only more so - eight megabytes of packed
# textures per file, and a format git can only ever store whole copies of.
#
# The two under assets/models-src/tiers/mine predate this line and are in the
# history raw. Leaving them: rewriting published history to save sixteen
# megabytes is a worse trade than carrying them.
*.blend filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
assets/icons-src/*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Normalise line endings. build.sh is run by bash and has to keep LF whatever
# a Windows checkout would otherwise do to it.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf

# Binary, so git never tries to normalise or diff them as text.
*.png binary
*.ttf binary
*.glb binary
*.blend binary
